WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.873
Conditional Matrix Flows for Gaussian Graphical
Models, from the University of Basel,

00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:07.990
unifies sparse network inference.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:13.764
With one trained matrix normalizing flow it
delivers the full Bayesian posterior for every

00:00:13.844 --> 00:00:16.972
regularization strength and every l-q pseudo-norm,

00:00:17.052 --> 00:00:19.794
the marginal likelihood for model selection,

00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:23.772
and the frequentist solution path through simulated
annealing.

00:00:24.152 --> 00:00:29.482
Gaussian Graphical Models read conditional
independence from the precision matrix,

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:35.222
the inverse covariance: a zero entry means two
variables are conditionally independent,

00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:38.520
so a sparse precision matrix recovers the network.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.534
The ideal l-zero penalty that counts edges is
combinatorial and intractable.

00:00:43.614 --> 00:00:47.625
Sub-l-one pseudo-norms approximate it well but are
non-convex,

00:00:47.705 --> 00:00:52.177
so most methods retreat to the convex l-one norm,
which over-shrinks,

00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:55.080
especially when variables outnumber samples.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.105
Two camps exist.

00:00:59.185 --> 00:01:02.831
The frequentist Graphical Lasso traces the whole
solution path as lambda varies,

00:01:02.911 --> 00:01:06.556
but returns only point estimates.

00:01:06.636 --> 00:01:10.281
The Bayesian Graphical Lasso gives a full posterior
and a marginal likelihood,

00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:14.007
but relies on Gibbs samplers that mix poorly and
must restart for every lambda.

00:01:14.087 --> 00:01:17.732
Variational inference could bridge them,

00:01:17.812 --> 00:01:21.457
yet the usual mean-field trick assumes independence,

00:01:21.537 --> 00:01:25.183
exactly what a graphical model seeks.

00:01:25.263 --> 00:01:28.908
We need a family that respects dependence and
flexes across lambda and the norm.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:32.361
The flow works on the Cholesky factorization.

00:01:32.441 --> 00:01:36.005
Any positive-definite matrix is L times L-transpose,

00:01:36.085 --> 00:01:41.961
so a d-dimensional precision matrix needs only d
times d plus one, over two, numbers.

00:01:42.041 --> 00:01:46.235
The flow pushes a base vector through
Sum-of-Sigmoids layers,

00:01:46.315 --> 00:01:52.051
reshapes it into a triangular matrix, forces the
diagonal positive with a softplus,

00:01:52.131 --> 00:01:54.223
and takes the Cholesky product,

00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:58.777
so every output is a valid precision matrix with
cheap Jacobians.

00:01:58.857 --> 00:02:01.370
A hypernetwork feeds in lambda and q,

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:08.657
and training minimizes the KL divergence to the
unnormalized posterior under a generalized-Normal prior.

00:02:08.737 --> 00:02:12.371
Lasso appears at q equals one, Ridge at q equals two,

00:02:12.451 --> 00:02:15.384
and smaller q reaches the sub-l-one regime.

00:02:15.764 --> 00:02:19.212
Experiments use two data types.

00:02:19.292 --> 00:02:22.740
First,

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.268
synthetic sparse precision matrices from
Scikit-learn: a small fifteen-by-fifteen case to visualize posteriors,

00:02:26.348 --> 00:02:29.796
and a thirty-dimensional setting with fifteen to
forty-five samples for edge recovery,

00:02:29.876 --> 00:02:33.324
averaged over ten ground-truth matrices.

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:36.852
Second,

00:02:36.932 --> 00:02:40.380
a real colorectal-cancer dataset with six clinical
variables and three hundred twelve

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.908
gene-expression measurements across one hundred
ninety patients,

00:02:43.988 --> 00:02:47.436
inferring only the relevant sub-blocks of the
precision matrix.

00:02:47.816 --> 00:02:51.197
One model does the work of many.

00:02:51.277 --> 00:02:54.658
Trained once,

00:02:54.738 --> 00:02:58.118
the flow reconstructs the frequentist solution path
with a mean squared error of just zero point zero five two.

00:02:58.198 --> 00:03:01.579
When samples are scarce,

00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:05.040
the sub-l-one pseudo-norms clearly beat the
Bayesian Graphical Lasso,

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.501
and the gain grows as q approaches zero.

00:03:08.581 --> 00:03:11.962
Against frequentist competitors it wins at every
sample size,

00:03:12.042 --> 00:03:15.422
most at fifteen samples.

00:03:15.502 --> 00:03:18.883
Its model selection picks a lambda of three point
five two,

00:03:18.963 --> 00:03:22.344
matching the cross-validated Graphical Lasso value
of three point three six.

00:03:22.724 --> 00:03:25.802
The key study varies the pseudo-norm exponent q.

00:03:25.882 --> 00:03:28.367
As q shrinks from one toward a quarter,

00:03:28.447 --> 00:03:32.973
the posterior median for a precision entry is
pulled less toward zero,

00:03:33.053 --> 00:03:35.341
reducing over-shrinkage as promised.

00:03:35.421 --> 00:03:42.183
At a lambda of zero point three the ninety-five
percent credible interval excludes zero for q below one,

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:47.775
while the Bayesian Lasso at q equals one still
includes zero and would drop the edge.

00:03:47.855 --> 00:03:49.814
A second knob, the temperature,

00:03:49.894 --> 00:03:54.156
slides the model from the Bayesian regime to the
frequentist path.

00:03:54.536 --> 00:03:58.178
The takeaway: one normalizing flow can replace a toolbox.

00:03:58.258 --> 00:04:05.035
Built on positive-definite matrices and conditioned
on both the shrinkage strength and the norm exponent,

00:04:05.115 --> 00:04:09.541
it delivers Bayesian posteriors,
marginal-likelihood model selection,

00:04:09.621 --> 00:04:14.242
and the frequentist solution path, for every
setting and every l-q norm,

00:04:14.322 --> 00:04:17.834
including the non-convex sub-l-one region others avoid.

00:04:17.914 --> 00:04:20.642
Edge recovery improves where it is hardest,

00:04:20.722 --> 00:04:24.168
and sampling runs hundreds of times faster than Gibbs.
